[section:fft Fast Fourier Transform]

[h4 Synopsis]

    #include <boost/math/tools/fft.hpp>

    namespace boost::math::tools {

    template <class Complex>
    class fft
    {
    public:
        using value_type = Complex;
        using real_type = /* the real type of Complex */;

        explicit fft(std::size_t n);

        std::size_t size() const noexcept;

        // In place.
        template <class ContiguousContainer>
        void transform(ContiguousContainer& v);

        template <class ContiguousContainer>
        void inverse_transform(ContiguousContainer& v);

        // Out of place: in is left unchanged.
        template <class InputContainer, class OutputContainer>
        void transform(InputContainer const& in, OutputContainer& out);

        template <class InputContainer, class OutputContainer>
        void inverse_transform(InputContainer const& in, OutputContainer& out);
    };

    template <class Complex>
    class real_fft
    {
    public:
        using value_type = Complex;
        using real_type = /* the real type of Complex */;

        explicit real_fft(std::size_t n);

        std::size_t size() const noexcept;

        // Reads n real values, overwrites the n/2 + 1 complex values of output.
        template <class RealContainer, class ContiguousComplexContainer>
        void transform(RealContainer const& input, ContiguousComplexContainer& output);
    };

    } // namespaces

[h4 Description]

`fft<Complex>` computes the discrete Fourier transform of a fixed length /n/, in place:

    using boost::math::tools::fft;
    std::vector<std::complex<double>> v = ...; // v.size() == n
    fft<std::complex<double>> f(v.size()); // builds the twiddle factors (and, for some n, Bluestein's chirp)
    f.transform(v);                         // v now holds the transform
    f.inverse_transform(v);                 // and now holds the original data again, up to rounding

The constructor does all the setup, so construct the object once and reuse it for every transform of that length.
It also allocates all the workspace a transform needs, so after construction no transform allocates memory.

The out-of-place overloads leave their input unchanged and write the transform to a second container of /n/ values,
which must not overlap the input:

    std::vector<std::complex<double>> X(v.size());
    f.transform(v, X);         // X = F(v), v is unchanged
    f.inverse_transform(X, v); // v = F^-1(X)

Any length /n/ > 0 is accepted; /n/ = 0 throws a `std::domain_error`, as does a container whose size is not /n/.

The template parameter is the complex type, which may be `std::complex<float>`, `std::complex<double>`, `std::complex<long double>`,
or a Boost.Multiprecision complex type such as `cpp_complex_50`, `cpp_complex_quad` or `mpc_complex_50`.
Its real type, `real_type`, is the type returned by its `real()` member: `T` for `std::complex<T>`, and the component type for a multiprecision complex number.
(`std::complex<cpp_bin_float_50>` is not supported: the standard leaves `std::complex` of anything but `float`, `double` and `long double` unspecified;
use `cpp_complex_50` instead.)
For a multiprecision complex type, include its header first: `<boost/multiprecision/cpp_complex.hpp>`, `<boost/multiprecision/complex128.hpp>` or `<boost/multiprecision/mpc.hpp>`.
Being generic in the floating point type is the main reason to use this rather than FFTW.

The data is passed as a container of `Complex` with contiguous storage, a `data()` member and a size, such as `std::vector<Complex>`, `std::array<Complex, N>` or `std::span<Complex>`.

`real_fft<Complex>` transforms /n/ real samples of type `real_type`. The caller supplies the output, which must hold exactly /n/\/2 + 1 values
(rounded down; otherwise a `std::domain_error` is thrown) and receives /X/[sub 0], ..., /X/[sub n/2]; the remaining coefficients are /X/[sub n-k] = conj(/X/[sub k]):

    using boost::math::tools::real_fft;
    using boost::multiprecision::cpp_bin_float_50;
    using boost::multiprecision::cpp_complex_50;
    std::vector<cpp_bin_float_50> x = ...;          // x.size() == n
    std::vector<cpp_complex_50> X(x.size() / 2 + 1);
    real_fft<cpp_complex_50> r(x.size());
    r.transform(x, X);

The input may be any container with `operator[]` and a size. Both may be `std::array`s.
For even /n/ the input is packed into a complex FFT of length /n/\/2, so it is about twice as fast as a complex transform of the real data.
For odd /n/ it simply uses a complex FFT of length /n/.

[h4 Conventions]

The forward transform uses the negative sign in the exponent and is not normalised:

[expression X[sub k] = [sum][sub j=0][super n-1] x[sub j] exp(-2[pi]/ijk/n)]

The inverse uses the positive sign and divides by /n/:

[expression x[sub j] = (1/n) [sum][sub k=0][super n-1] X[sub k] exp(2[pi]/ijk/n)]

so that `inverse_transform` undoes `transform`.
This is the same convention as FFTW's `FFTW_FORWARD` (whose backward transform is not normalised), numpy's `fft`/`ifft`, and Matlab's `fft`/`ifft`.

[h4 Thread safety]

An `fft` or `real_fft` object holds its own workspace, which every transform writes to, so `transform` and `inverse_transform` are not `const`
and an object must not be used by two threads at once. As with an FFTW plan and its buffers, give each thread its own object;
different objects, even of the same length, are independent.

[h4 Algorithms and complexity]

Lengths whose prime factors are all at most 97 use a mixed-radix Cooley--Tukey FFT in Stockham's autosort form,
with radix-4 stages where possible and then one stage for each odd prime factor; it ping-pongs between the data and a scratch buffer of length /n/,
so no digit reversal is needed. Radices 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 11 and 13 have dedicated butterflies; larger primes /p/ use a generic one,
which costs ['O(p)] per point. The cutoff of 97 is where Bluestein's algorithm (below) starts to win:
in double precision, for /n/ = /p/[times]2[super k] and /p/[times]1000, Bluestein is faster from /p/ = 97 to 127 on, depending on /n/,
and the mixed-radix FFT is also the more accurate up to about the same /p/.
Every other length uses Bluestein's algorithm, which rewrites the length-/n/ DFT as a convolution and evaluates it with two mixed-radix FFTs of length /m/ >= 2/n/ - 1,
where /m/ is the smallest 2[super a]3[super b]5[super c] >= 2/n/ - 1 (or the next power of two, if that is within 20%).
Lengths that are a smooth number times a large prime, such as 2[times]1009, are also done by Bluestein over the whole length:
applying Bluestein only to the large prime factor inside the mixed-radix decomposition would save at most about a factor of 2, at the cost of a second code path.
Both algorithms are ['O(n log n)] in time.
A mixed-radix `fft` stores at most 2/n/ complex twiddle factors and a workspace of /n/ more;
a Bluestein `fft` stores about 6/m/ complex numbers, including a workspace of 2/m/. A `real_fft` of odd length stores another /n/.
A Bluestein transform does two FFTs of length /m/ >= 2/n/, so it is roughly 5 to 10 times slower than a mixed-radix transform of similar length.
If you are free to choose /n/, choose one whose prime factors are 2, 3 and 5, preferably a power of two.

[h4 Accuracy]

Every twiddle factor exp(-2[pi]/ik/n) is computed directly, never by a recurrence: the angle is reduced to the first octant in integer arithmetic,
and the sine and cosine of what remains are computed by `sin_pi` and `cos_pi`, so each twiddle is accurate to an ulp or two.
With accurate twiddles, the FFT has normwise relative error ['O([epsilon][radic]log n)] for random data,
and the largest error in any single coefficient is ['O([epsilon] log n)] relative to the root mean square of the coefficients.

The table below gives errors in units of the type's [epsilon], measured against a reference computed in 50 decimal digits
(an ['O(n[super 2])] DFT up to /n/ = 1031, our FFT in `cpp_complex_50` beyond that) for uniformly random complex data in the unit square
(the rows for /n/ = 17 and 101 are means over 20 random vectors, against a 100-digit DFT).
"rms" is ||/X/ - /X/[sub ref]||[sub 2] / ||/X/[sub ref]||[sub 2] and "max" is the largest |/X/[sub k] - /X/[sub ref,k]| divided by the root mean square of /X/[sub ref].
FFTW 3 (double, `FFTW_ESTIMATE`) is given for comparison.
On Apple silicon, `long double` is `double`, and gives the same errors.

[table:fft_accuracy Errors in units of epsilon
