For `cpp_complex_50` (against a 100 digit reference), the mixed-radix FFT gives rms errors of 0.4[epsilon] at /n/ = 12, 1.3[epsilon] at /n/ = 100,
1.7[epsilon] at /n/ = 1000 and 4096 and 1.8[epsilon] at /n/ = 3125,
and Bluestein gives 2.9[epsilon] at /n/ = 101, 3.5[epsilon] at /n/ = 2018 and 3.3[epsilon] at /n/ = 4099.

The mixed-radix FFT is as accurate as FFTW.
Bluestein's algorithm loses a little: its rms and worst errors are about twice those of a mixed-radix FFT of similar length,
and they still grow like [radic]log /n/ and log /n/ respectively.
Prime factors up to 97 are transformed directly by a butterfly, which is as accurate as FFTW (at /n/ = 17 the errors are the same);
for larger primes, where this FFT uses Bluestein's algorithm, the two are comparable.
The round trip `inverse_transform(transform(x))` reproduces /x/ to about the same accuracy as the forward transform.

These are errors for data whose coefficients all have similar size.
The error bounds are normwise, so coefficients much smaller than the rms coefficient have large relative errors; that is inherent in any FFT.

[h4 Performance]

On an Apple M1 Pro core with clang -O2, in double precision (google/benchmark, CPU time medians of 7), against FFTW 3.3.11 with `FFTW_ESTIMATE` plans.
`FFTW_MEASURE` plans are faster to execute, but making one runs and times many candidate transforms, which costs far more than the transforms it saves,
unless the same plan is reused very many times; `FFTW_ESTIMATE` is the planner that, like this FFT's constructor, does no measurement.
The FFTW is Homebrew's arm64 build, which is configured without NEON, so it runs FFTW's scalar codelets;
an FFTW built with SIMD would be noticeably faster than the figures below.
Each comparison is like for like: in place against an in-place plan, out of place against an out-of-place plan, and neither allocates during a transform.

