For lengths below about 10[super 4] this FFT takes about the same time as `FFTW_ESTIMATE` up to about 1.5 times as long (up to 2 times for the shortest complex lengths, and 2.8 times for a real transform of length 64),
and for longer lengths it is usually faster, by up to a factor of 2.
Lengths with a large prime factor below the cutoff of 97, such as 97[times]2[super 6], take about 1.7 times as long as FFTW,
and a real transform of odd length, which is done as a complex transform of the same length, takes 2 to 3 times as long when the length is short.
The aim of this implementation is a generic `Real`, no external dependency, and accuracy; for the fastest double-precision transforms, use a SIMD build of FFTW.

[h4 References]

* J. W. Cooley and J. W. Tukey, ['An algorithm for the machine calculation of complex Fourier series], Math. Comp. 19 (1965), 297--301.
* L. I. Bluestein, ['A linear filtering approach to the computation of the discrete Fourier transform], IEEE Trans. Audio Electroacoust. 18 (1970), 451--455.
* N. J. Higham, ['Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms], 2nd ed., SIAM 2002, section 24.1.
* J. C. Schatzman, ['Accuracy of the discrete Fourier transform and the fast Fourier transform], SIAM J. Sci. Comput. 17 (1996), 1150--1166.

[endsect] [/section:fft Fast Fourier Transform]
